## Design Notes

- Clean, minimal. No stock photos of handshakes or office workers.
- Dark background with light text or light background with dark text. Pick one and commit.
- Your profile photo somewhere on the Home page. Same one from LinkedIn.
- Mobile-first. Most prospects will check this on their phone.
- Fast. No heavy animations or videos on load. A business owner on a job site with spotty service needs it to load in under 2 seconds.
- No chat widgets, popups, or cookie banners. Keep it clean.
- Service sections should feel like conversations, not brochures.

---

## Content Rules

- Every section starts with the business owner's problem. The solution comes second.
- Write the way Paul talks. Short sentences. Direct. No fluff.
- Address the reader as "you" throughout. Never talk about "clients" or "businesses" in the third person.
- No em dashes, semicolons, or colons in body copy.
- No buzzwords. No "synergy," "leverage," "cutting-edge," "results-driven."
- No stock phrases. No "I am passionate about helping businesses grow."
- Every sentence should pass the test: would a business owner read this and think "that is exactly my problem"?
- Do not use emojis anywhere on the site.